# Curb V1 implementation plan

**Purpose:** build a narrow, production-quality vertical slice that turns a signed agreement's explicitly selected, supported AI/data-handling commitments into reproducible engineering checks, findings, evidence, and re-verification.  
**Planning status:** implementation plan, not legal advice or a compliance certification.  
**Prepared:** 13 October 2026 (Europe/London).  
**Initial proving ground:** a pinned, isolated fork of [Vercel Chatbot](https://github.com/vercel/chatbot), formerly AI Chatbot.  
**Illustrative source:** [Common Paper AI Addendum v1.0](https://commonpaper.com/standards/ai-addendum/1.0), only as an open, versioned example of agreement structure and terminology.

## 1. Decision and product position

Build Curb as an **evidence-based contract-control verifier for a small, declared catalog of machine-testable commitments**. It should answer: “For this agreement snapshot and this repository revision, what evidence supports or contradicts each supported control?” It should not answer: “Is this company legally compliant?”

The first usable experience is intentionally small:

1. An authorized customer connects one repository and uploads or selects one agreement snapshot.
2. A lawyer or authorized operator selects and confirms supported commitments from the extracted text; Curb never silently treats model output as a binding obligation.
3. Curb maps each confirmed commitment to one or more versioned technical controls.
4. A deterministic scan produces pass, fail, unknown, or not-applicable, with code/config evidence and a reproducible run manifest.
5. A developer fixes a finding, Curb reruns the same control set against the new immutable commit, and the report shows the delta.

This is a developer-facing assurance workflow, not a contract authoring product, legal opinion engine, general-purpose GRC suite, runtime DLP system, or automated regulator submission service. The positioning must say “checks configured engineering evidence against confirmed contract requirements,” never “ensures compliance.”

### Feasibility is not demand

The Vercel fixture can demonstrate technical feasibility, evaluator quality, workflow usefulness, and repeatability. It cannot demonstrate that customers will pay, that legal teams accept the workflow, that a contract category is commercially urgent, or that findings alter procurement outcomes. Those are product-market-demand hypotheses. Maintain separate evidence tracks:

| Claim | Evidence required | Decision owner |
|---|---|---|
| A supported control can be checked accurately | Locked Curb Bench cases; unseen holdout performance | Engineering + security |
| Findings are actionable | Time-to-remediate and developer usability study | Product + engineering |
| Customers value the workflow | Design-partner interviews, pilots, willingness-to-pay signal | Founder/product |
| Agreement interpretation is sound | Counsel-approved policy cards and review workflow | Qualified counsel |

No technical benchmark score may be presented as proof of legal compliance or market demand.

